// The Slack library: a Socket Mode connection capability plus the chat surface — the message plane
// (`watch_messages` in, `send_message` / `try_send` out) and the interaction plane (`ask`). The
// externals are implemented in `slack.ts` (the same module path, so they register under `slack.*`).
// The one-line integration is the provider:
// `use slack.provider(bot_source = credentials.env(key = "SLACK_BOT_TOKEN"), app_source = credentials.env(key = "SLACK_APP_TOKEN"))`.
//
// Socket Mode needs no public URL and no request-signature verification: the sidecar opens an outbound
// WebSocket with the app-level token (`xapp-…`) and Slack pushes events over it, while the bot token
// (`xoxb-…`) drives the Web API (posting, uploads, attachment downloads, dialogs). Both tokens stay
// inside the sidecar, keyed by the opaque client handle — they are never served as a value.
//
// Files are first-class on both directions: an incoming message's attachments arrive as `file` values
// (the sidecar downloads each from Slack's authenticated file URL and uploads it over the blob side
// channel), and `send_message` posts `file` values back as Slack uploads (`files.uploadV2`).
//
// This module is the Slack half of a TWIN CONTRACT with the `discord` package: the same data types
// with the same fields, so a bot ports between them by swapping the import. Two divergences are
// deliberate and documented where they appear: `message.thread` / `send_message`'s @thread_ts@ (Slack
// addresses a thread by its parent's `ts`, which is also a message's identity) and the `slack_error`
// classification (Slack's error strings rather than Discord's HTTP statuses).

@"The live Slack connection (the Socket Mode and Web API clients live in the sidecar; the handle is
opaque)."
request connection() -> string

@"Provide the Slack connection for the extent of the continuation: resolve the two sources ONCE (a
socket credential is fixed at connect — a rotation lands on the next restart, not mid-connection),
connect over Socket Mode, then serve `connection` to everything downstream. A failed connect (a bad
`xapp-…` token, a transient network fault) raises `slack_error` (`auth_error` | `api_error`) —
surfacing here rather than on the first send. Slack genuinely takes TWO credentials, so there are two
sources: the bot token (`xoxb-…`, the Web API) and the app-level token (`xapp-…`, the socket)."
agent provider[R, effect E](
  @"Where the bot token (`xoxb-…`) lives; resolved once at connect." bot_source: credentials.source,
  @"Where the app-level token (`xapp-…`) lives; resolved once at connect." app_source: credentials.source,
  @"Runs with `connection` served; its result is the provider's result." continuation: agent (value: null) -> R with {...E, connection},
) -> R with E | io | prelude.throw[slack_error | env.missing_secret | oauth.server_error] {
  let client = create_slack_client(bot_token = credentials.resolve(source = bot_source), app_token = credentials.resolve(source = app_source))
  // Bind the socket's lifetime to this run: however the provider ends — normal completion, cancel, or
  // the continuation unwinding — close the connection on the way out. A socket left open keeps
  // receiving events, and Slack round-robins each event across every socket open on the app token, so a
  // dead run's zombie socket would swallow messages (acking them) that a live bot then never sees.
  finally { slack_close(client = client) }
  use handler {
    request connection() { next client }
  }
  continuation(value = null)
}

@"Connect to Slack — open the Socket Mode WebSocket and the Web API client — and return an opaque
client handle. Raises `slack_error` (`auth_error` | `api_error`) when the connect fails — a bad
app-level token is `auth_error`, a transient network fault `api_error`. Prefer the provider."
external agent create_slack_client(
  @"The bot token (`xoxb-…`) every Web API call authenticates with." bot_token: string of private,
  @"The app-level token (`xapp-…`) the Socket Mode WebSocket opens with." app_token: string of private,
) -> string with prelude.throw[slack_error]

@"Close a Slack connection: disconnect the Socket Mode WebSocket and drop the client handle. The
provider arms this as a `finally`, so the connection dies with the run — a socket left open keeps
receiving events, and Slack round-robins each event across every socket open on the app token, so a
dead run's zombie socket would swallow messages (acking them) that a live bot then never sees.
Idempotent: closing an unknown or already-closed handle is a no-op, since a finalizer may run more
than once."
external agent slack_close(
  @"The opaque handle `create_slack_client` returned." client: string,
) -> null

@"An authentication / authorization failure: the bot token is invalid, revoked, or missing a scope the
call needs (Slack `invalid_auth`, `token_revoked`, `missing_scope`, …); @message@ carries Slack's error
string. The bot cannot recover on its own — the operator must fix the token or scopes and restart the
run (the connection is fixed at start). Let it surface so the bot stops loudly rather than silently
dropping every reply."
data auth_error(message: string)

@"A Slack API call failed for a non-auth reason: a rate limit, a transient network fault, a channel the
bot is not in, a message too long, a Block Kit payload over one of Slack's size caps; @message@ carries
Slack's error string. Usually per-message or transient, so a bot catches it to drop just that reply and
keep serving."
data api_error(message: string)

// A Slack failure: an auth/authorization failure or a plain API failure. Raised as a `prelude.throw`
// (never a panic — that would tear the whole run down), classified in the sidecar by Slack's error
// code. The Discord twin has the same two constructors, classified by HTTP status instead. (Type
// synonyms take no docs.)
type slack_error = auth_error | api_error

// ─── the message plane ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

@"One incoming message, as `watch_messages` delivers it. @author@ is the sender's raw Slack user id (an
opaque `U…` id, not a name); tag it with `crypto.hmac_sha256` under a secret key before letting it leave
the program, e.g. as an AI provider's abuse-attribution tag. @thread@ is the thread the message was
posted in, or `null` for a top-level message — pass it straight back as `send_message`'s @thread_ts@ to
reply where the message came from. (@thread@ is the Slack twin's one extra field: Discord has no `ts`
that doubles as a thread address.)"
data message(
  @"The channel the message was posted in." channel: string,
  @"The sender's raw Slack user id (`U…`)." author: string,
  @"The message text as Slack delivered it (mrkdwn)." text: string,
  @"The message's attachments, downloaded as `file` values." files: array[file],
  @"The `ts` of the thread this message belongs to, or `null` for a top-level message." thread: string | null,
)

@"Low-level: post to a channel on a given client — a thread reply when @thread_ts@ carries a `ts`, a
channel post when it is `null`; any files upload as attachments — returning the posted message's `ts`
(a plain post's own `ts`, a file upload's share `ts`, or `\"\"` when Slack reports none). Raises
`auth_error` or `api_error` (`slack_error`) when the Slack API call fails. Prefer the send_message agent."
external agent slack_send(
  @"The opaque handle `create_slack_client` returned." client: string,
  @"The id of the channel to post in (e.g. \"C0123456789\")." channel: string,
  @"The message text (Slack mrkdwn); with files it becomes their caption." text: string,
  @"The parent message's `ts` to reply in its thread, or `null` to post to the channel." thread_ts: string | null,
  @"Files to upload as the message's attachments (`[]` for a plain text post)." files: array[file],
) -> string with prelude.throw[slack_error]

@"Low-level: listen on a channel and deliver each incoming message to a callback. Prefer the
watch_messages agent."
external agent slack_watch[effect E](
  @"The opaque handle `create_slack_client` returned." client: string,
  @"The id of the channel to listen on; messages elsewhere are ignored." channel: string,
  @"Called once per incoming message; its effects E flow to the caller's handlers." deliver_to: agent (message: message) -> null with E,
) -> never with E

@"Post a message to a channel using the ambient Slack client, returning the posted MESSAGE'S `ts` — the
composable seam a later edit / thread-reply agent will address a message by, so a caller that needs to
refer back keeps it (and one that does not simply drops it). Pass a message's `ts` as @thread_ts@ to
reply in its thread (a delivered `message.thread` passed straight back replies where it came from);
@files@ upload as attachments and default to none. Raises `slack_error` (`auth_error` | `api_error`)
when the send fails — catch `api_error` to keep a bot serving past a transient failure, and let
`auth_error` surface so a bad token stops the bot loudly."
agent send_message(
  @"The id of the channel to post in (e.g. \"C0123456789\")." channel: string,
  @"The message text (Slack mrkdwn); with files it becomes their caption." text: string,
  @"Files to upload as the message's attachments; defaults to none." files: array[file] ?= [],
  @"The parent message's `ts` to reply in its thread; omit (or pass `null`) to post to the channel." thread_ts: string | null ?= null,
) -> string {
  slack_send(client = connection(), channel = channel, text = text, thread_ts = thread_ts, files = files)
}

@"Post RESILIENTLY, fire-and-forget — the composition every long-lived bot writes around
`send_message`, packaged: a blank @text@ with no @files@ posts nothing (a quiet protocol's silence);
a transient `api_error` (a rate limit, a channel the bot is not in) drops just this post so the bot
keeps serving; `auth_error` re-raises so a bad token still stops the bot loudly. The posted message's
`ts` is deliberately dropped — a caller that needs it wants `send_message` and its own error policy."
agent try_send(
  @"The id of the channel to post in (e.g. \"C0123456789\")." channel: string,
  @"The message text; blank with no files posts nothing." text: string,
  @"Files to upload as attachments; defaults to none." files: array[file] ?= [],
  @"The parent message's `ts` to reply in its thread; omit (or pass `null`) to post to the channel." thread_ts: string | null ?= null,
) -> null {
  if (string.is_blank(value = text) && array.length(target = files) == 0) {
    null
  } else {
    use handler {
      request prelude.throw(error: slack_error) -> never {
        match (error) {
          case api_error(message => _) -> { break null }
          // The residual is exactly `auth_error` — rethrown as bound, no reconstruction.
          case rest -> { prelude.throw(error = rest) }
        }
      }
    }
    let _message_ts = send_message(channel = channel, text = text, files = files, thread_ts = thread_ts)
    null
  }
}

@"Serve a channel forever, delivering each incoming `message` to @deliver_to@ — shaped like
`time.watch` / `google_calendar.watch` so it composes under `parallel [ … ]`. The bot's own posts (and
other bots') are not delivered, so replying from @deliver_to@ cannot loop. Delivery is at-least-once:
Slack re-sends an event whose acknowledgement was lost, and no dedup memory is kept here."
agent watch_messages[effect E](
  @"The id of the channel to listen on (e.g. \"C0123456789\")." channel: string,
  @"Called once per incoming message; its effects E flow to the caller's handlers." deliver_to: agent (message: message) -> null with E,
) -> never with E | connection | io {
  slack_watch(client = connection(), channel = channel, deliver_to = deliver_to)
}

// ─── the interaction plane ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

@"A pressable BUTTON. @label@ is what the channel reads; @id@ is what comes back on `clicked`, so a
program branches on the key it chose and never on display text. Slack caps a button's label at 75
characters — an over-cap control is rejected by the platform as `api_error`."
data button(
  @"The stable key this control answers with; matched as `clicked`'s @id@." id: string,
  @"The button's visible text (≤75 characters)." label: string,
)

@"A DROPDOWN of fixed options: choosing one answers the ask with `chose`, carrying the option's text
verbatim (so a program may match the option itself — the options are its own strings). Slack caps the
placeholder and each option at 75 characters."
data select(
  @"The stable key this control answers with; matched as `chose`'s @id@." id: string,
  @"The dropdown's placeholder text, shown before anything is chosen (≤75 characters)." label: string,
  @"The options offered; the chosen one comes back verbatim (each ≤75 characters)." options: array[string],
)

@"One text input inside a `form`. @value@ is a PREFILL, which is what makes DRAFT EDITING an ordinary
`ask`: ship the draft as @value@ and the human edits it in place. Every field must be filled — Slack's
own client refuses an empty input, and that is the only validation there is (see `ask`)."
data field(
  @"The key this field's text is stored under in `submitted`'s @values@." id: string,
  @"The label shown above the input." label: string,
  @"The text the input opens with — a prefill the human edits. Defaults to an empty input." value: string ?= "",
  @"A multi-line textarea instead of a single line. Defaults to single-line." multiline: boolean ?= false,
)

@"A FORM: a button in the channel that opens a DIALOG of text inputs, answering the ask with
`submitted` once the human submits it. This is how free text is collected — a one-field form asks an
open question, a prefilled multi-field form reviews a draft. @title@ is capped at 24 characters, the
tighter of the two platforms' caps, so a twin program ports unchanged."
data form(
  @"The stable key this control answers with; matched as `submitted`'s @id@." id: string,
  @"The visible text of the button that OPENS the dialog (≤75 characters)." label: string,
  @"The dialog's own heading (≤24 characters)." title: string,
  @"The inputs the dialog collects, in order." fields: array[field],
)

// One control offered under an `ask`'s prompt. The three shapes are the three ways a human answers —
// press, choose, write — and each has exactly one matching `answer` constructor. (Type synonyms take
// no docs.)
type control = button | select | form

@"A `button` was pressed: @id@ is that button's own id, @by@ the Slack user id of whoever pressed it."
data clicked(
  @"The `button`'s @id@." id: string,
  @"The answerer's raw Slack user id (`U…`)." by: string,
)

@"A `select`'s option was chosen: @id@ is that select's own id and @option@ the chosen text verbatim."
data chose(
  @"The `select`'s @id@." id: string,
  @"The chosen option, exactly as it was offered." option: string,
  @"The answerer's raw Slack user id (`U…`)." by: string,
)

@"A `form` was submitted: @id@ is that form's own id and @values@ maps each `field`'s id to the text the
human left in it (`record.get` reads one)."
data submitted(
  @"The `form`'s @id@." id: string,
  @"Each `field`'s id to the text submitted for it." values: record[string],
  @"The answerer's raw Slack user id (`U…`)." by: string,
)

// What an `ask` came back with: one constructor per `control` shape, so matching the answer is the same
// dispatch as choosing the control. (Type synonyms take no docs.)
type answer = clicked | chose | submitted

@"Post @prompt@ with @controls@ under it and BLOCK until someone in the channel answers, returning the
`answer` that control produced — the ONE interaction primitive, so the four human-in-the-loop shapes are
four control lists rather than four agents: an APPROVAL is two `button`s, an OPEN QUESTION is a
one-field `form`, a DRAFT REVIEW is a `form` whose `field` is prefilled with the draft (with a `button`
beside it to reject), and a MULTIPLE CHOICE is a `select`. Every control is live at once and the FIRST
answer settles the ask; the controls are then stripped from the message, so a second answer has nothing
to press. Branch on the control's own id — `case clicked(id => \"approve\", by => _)` — never on display
text.

Post to a channel whose MEMBERSHIP is the trust boundary: any member may answer. @by@ is the answerer's
raw Slack user id (an opaque `U…` id, not a name); tag it with `crypto.hmac_sha256` under a secret key
before letting it leave the program.

Opening a `form`'s dialog and closing it again is NOT an answer: nothing was submitted, so the question
stays open and any control — that form again, or a sibling — can still answer it. A curious press cannot
consume the ask.

The ask holds NO time limit, deliberately: a deadline is `time.with_deadline` wrapped around it, and a
withdrawal is `region.cancel_by_id` on the fiber holding it. One blocking question composes with both,
whereas an expiry baked in here would make every caller handle a timeout it never asked for.

A `form`'s inputs are NOT validated here. Slack's client requires each input to be non-empty and that is
the whole of it: the socket acknowledges every interaction the instant it arrives — which is exactly what
closes the dialog — so this package cannot answer a submission with per-field errors. Validate in the
program and `ask` again.

The wait rides one external call, so a runtime restart while it is open interrupts it under the
at-most-once rule (a catchable panic) and the posted controls go stale. That failure is the DESIGNED
outcome, not a defect to absorb here: let the panic ride to your supervisor and restart the asking flow —
whoever wanted the answer asks again — rather than guessing an answer that never landed. Raises
`slack_error` when the prompt cannot be posted, or when a `form`'s dialog cannot be opened (its
three-second interaction token expired, a size cap exceeded); catch `api_error` to ask again."
agent ask(
  @"The id of the channel to ask in — its membership is who may answer." channel: string,
  @"The question posted above the controls (Slack mrkdwn)." prompt: string,
  @"What the human may answer with: `button` / `select` / `form` values, rendered in one row in order. Each control's @id@ must be DISTINCT within one ask — the id IS the correlation key Slack carries back, so a repeat would make two controls indistinguishable (Slack rejects the duplicate outright)." controls: array[control],
) -> answer with connection | io | prelude.throw[slack_error] {
  slack_ask(client = connection(), channel = channel, prompt = prompt, controls = controls)
}

@"Low-level: post a prompt with its controls on a given client and await the first answer. Prefer the
ask agent."
external agent slack_ask(
  @"The opaque handle `create_slack_client` returned." client: string,
  @"The id of the channel to ask in." channel: string,
  @"The question posted above the controls." prompt: string,
  @"The controls offered under the prompt." controls: array[control],
) -> answer with prelude.throw[slack_error]
